[{:db/id 17592186124598 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f43706f1-2fe0-517f-8d0c-63c125c3a2af" "question-a9a20426-c841-5b8d-947d-6ac99fc75818"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-f43706f1-2fe0-517f-8d0c-63c125c3a2af" :question/id #uuid "f43706f1-2fe0-517f-8d0c-63c125c3a2af" :question/math-academy-id "q-97014" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -7 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ is a diagonalization of the matrix $A.$ So let's apply the diagonalization algorithm to $A.$\n\n**Step 1.** From the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-1\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=-11.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- Consider $\\lambda =-1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -7 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have two linearly independent eigenvectors\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Consider $\\lambda =-11.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-11)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -7 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]-(-11)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking for nonzero solutions of $(A-(-11)I)x=0,$ we obtain the eigenvector \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the invertible matrix $P$ using our eigenvectors, as follows:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c3218ef6-5b7b-528a-b8e6-88a8580ab11f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c3218ef6-5b7b-528a-b8e6-88a8580ab11f" :answer-field/id #uuid "c3218ef6-5b7b-528a-b8e6-88a8580ab11f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5e11a990-816a-542c-b5ce-f74191043b00" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4fa9c7fe-f9c8-5b2a-a36e-c88f5fd1d690" "answer-7f072d48-3066-5492-ab6f-4730a7f2eb08" "answer-5e11a990-816a-542c-b5ce-f74191043b00" "answer-89e0cbde-a167-5f48-9072-964a52bddbff" "answer-c12409d5-4f99-5632-a15a-aefd07c8d159"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4fa9c7fe-f9c8-5b2a-a36e-c88f5fd1d690" :answer/id #uuid "4fa9c7fe-f9c8-5b2a-a36e-c88f5fd1d690" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f072d48-3066-5492-ab6f-4730a7f2eb08" :answer/id #uuid "7f072d48-3066-5492-ab6f-4730a7f2eb08" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e11a990-816a-542c-b5ce-f74191043b00" :answer/id #uuid "5e11a990-816a-542c-b5ce-f74191043b00" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89e0cbde-a167-5f48-9072-964a52bddbff" :answer/id #uuid "89e0cbde-a167-5f48-9072-964a52bddbff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c12409d5-4f99-5632-a15a-aefd07c8d159" :answer/id #uuid "c12409d5-4f99-5632-a15a-aefd07c8d159" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a9a20426-c841-5b8d-947d-6ac99fc75818" :question/id #uuid "a9a20426-c841-5b8d-947d-6ac99fc75818" :question/math-academy-id "q-140307" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ is a diagonalization of the matrix $A.$ So let's apply the diagonalization algorithm to $A.$\n\n**Step 1.** From the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-7\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=7.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- Consider $\\lambda =-7.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]-(-7)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 12 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -6 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 12 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -6 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-6{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have two linearly independent eigenvectors\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Consider $\\lambda =7.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-7I & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]-7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-15 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 6 & -11\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking for nonzero solutions of $(A-7I)x=0,$ we obtain the eigenvector \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the invertible matrix $P$ using our eigenvectors, as follows:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ea102311-30b8-53f8-a714-5a03eab37db6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ea102311-30b8-53f8-a714-5a03eab37db6" :answer-field/id #uuid "ea102311-30b8-53f8-a714-5a03eab37db6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8437c784-c077-5f6f-aef5-e79f5e51b2dd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-94ef147b-5990-5977-8014-9ea82929319e" "answer-8437c784-c077-5f6f-aef5-e79f5e51b2dd" "answer-fb316a6f-915c-5799-a609-85148fb62007" "answer-290c2d0a-3e1a-5fa6-bb40-54236727b6e2" "answer-652117fd-52b1-56a6-bd76-aac74686d76a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94ef147b-5990-5977-8014-9ea82929319e" :answer/id #uuid "94ef147b-5990-5977-8014-9ea82929319e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-17 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8437c784-c077-5f6f-aef5-e79f5e51b2dd" :answer/id #uuid "8437c784-c077-5f6f-aef5-e79f5e51b2dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb316a6f-915c-5799-a609-85148fb62007" :answer/id #uuid "fb316a6f-915c-5799-a609-85148fb62007" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-290c2d0a-3e1a-5fa6-bb40-54236727b6e2" :answer/id #uuid "290c2d0a-3e1a-5fa6-bb40-54236727b6e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-652117fd-52b1-56a6-bd76-aac74686d76a" :answer/id #uuid "652117fd-52b1-56a6-bd76-aac74686d76a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-afea8b61-c022-5abe-9036-47744d7d